      "side": 1,
      "message": "For this particular change `struct pkvm_memcache tmp_mc \u003d READ_ONCE(*host_mc);`, READ_ONCE is defined as below for x86:\n```\n  /*\n   * Yes, this permits 64-bit accesses on 32-bit architectures. These will\n   * actually be atomic in some cases (namely Armv7 + LPAE), but for others we\n   * rely on the access being split into 2x32-bit accesses for a 32-bit quantity\n   * (e.g. a virtual address) and a strong prevailing wind.\n   */\n  #define compiletime_assert_rwonce_type(t)                                       \\\n          compiletime_assert(__native_word(t) || sizeof(t) \u003d\u003d sizeof(long long),  \\\n                  \"Unsupported access size for {READ,WRITE}_ONCE().\")\n\n  /*\n   * Use __READ_ONCE() instead of READ_ONCE() if you do not require any\n   * atomicity. Note that this may result in tears!\n   */\n  #ifndef __READ_ONCE\n  #define __READ_ONCE(x)  (*(const volatile __unqual_scalar_typeof(x) *)\u0026(x))\n  #endif\n\n  #define READ_ONCE(x)                                                    \\\n  ({                                                                      \\\n          compiletime_assert_rwonce_type(x);                              \\\n          __READ_ONCE(x);                                                 \\\n  })\n```\nIt seems the type of x should be scalar type but host_mc isn\u0027t, so using READ_ONCE(*host_mc) will trigger a compiling error?\n\n\u003e For the record, pKVM-ARM also seems to extensively use READ_ONCE in such cases, probably for the same reason, for example in init_pkvm_hyp_vcpu():\n\nWhile looking at pKVM-ARM code, seems it doesn\u0027t strictly use READ_ONCE for every read of the shared structures?\n```\n  static void flush_hyp_vcpu(struct pkvm_hyp_vcpu *hyp_vcpu)\n  {\n          struct kvm_vcpu *host_vcpu \u003d hyp_vcpu-\u003ehost_vcpu;\n\n          fpsimd_sve_flush();\n          flush_debug_state(hyp_vcpu);\n\n          hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.ctxt        \u003d host_vcpu-\u003earch.ctxt;\n\n          hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.mdcr_el2    \u003d host_vcpu-\u003earch.mdcr_el2;\n          hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.hcr_el2 \u0026\u003d ~(HCR_TWI | HCR_TWE);\n          hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.hcr_el2 |\u003d READ_ONCE(host_vcpu-\u003earch.hcr_el2) \u0026\n                                                   (HCR_TWI | HCR_TWE);\n\n          hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.iflags      \u003d host_vcpu-\u003earch.iflags;\n\n          hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.vsesr_el2   \u003d host_vcpu-\u003earch.vsesr_el2;\n\n          hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.vgic_cpu.vgic_v3 \u003d host_vcpu-\u003earch.vgic_cpu.vgic_v3;\n  }\n```\n\nThe core benefit of using A\u003dREAD_ONCE(B) I got from gemini is that: Prevent Load Tearing, Prevent Load Omission (Caching in Registers) and Prevent Code Reordering. And also asked her about the security risk without using READ_ONCE, and this seems a valid point:\n\u003e2. The Security Threat: Register Spilling of Stale ReadsIf the compiler reads a multi-word or large structure from the host without READ_ONCE(), it treats that data as plain, stable variables.The Exploit ScenarioIf your handler loads a few configuration flags from the Host using plain assignments:\ncuint64_t flag1 \u003d host_vcpu-\u003eflag1;\nuint64_t flag2 \u003d host_vcpu-\u003eflag2;\nUse code with caution.If the function gets interrupted or encounters register pressure downstream, the compiler may spill flag1 or flag2 onto the hypervisor\u0027s secure stack (rsp).If a malicious Host managed to inject malformed or malicious data into those flags right before the spill, the hypervisor writes un-sanitized, Host-controlled payloads directly into its secure stack frames. If there is a separate code path vulnerability (like a minor buffer overflow or stack-read bug), the Host can use this control to map out or attack the hypervisor stack structure.\n\nIf so, then seems all the read from shared kvm/kvm_vcpu should use READ_ONCE?\n\n\u003e ...Also wondering: should we similarly use WRITE_ONCE whenever writing data to shared_vcpu or shared_kvm (to prevent some other security issues)? According to gemini, we should:\n\nIt seems makes sense. But seems pKVM-ARM doesn\u0027t strictly use WRITE_ONCE?\n\n```\n  static void sync_hyp_vcpu(struct pkvm_hyp_vcpu *hyp_vcpu)\n  {\n          struct kvm_vcpu *host_vcpu \u003d hyp_vcpu-\u003ehost_vcpu;\n          struct vgic_v3_cpu_if *hyp_cpu_if \u003d \u0026hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.vgic_cpu.vgic_v3;\n          struct vgic_v3_cpu_if *host_cpu_if \u003d \u0026host_vcpu-\u003earch.vgic_cpu.vgic_v3;\n          unsigned int i;\n\n          fpsimd_sve_sync(\u0026hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu);\n          sync_debug_state(hyp_vcpu);\n\n          host_vcpu-\u003earch.ctxt            \u003d hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.ctxt;\n\n          host_vcpu-\u003earch.hcr_el2         \u003d hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.hcr_el2;\n\n          host_vcpu-\u003earch.fault           \u003d hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.fault;\n\n          host_vcpu-\u003earch.iflags          \u003d hyp_vcpu-\u003evcpu.arch.iflags;\n\n          host_cpu_if-\u003evgic_hcr           \u003d hyp_cpu_if-\u003evgic_hcr;\n          for (i \u003d 0; i \u003c hyp_cpu_if-\u003eused_lrs; ++i)\n                  host_cpu_if-\u003evgic_lr[i] \u003d hyp_cpu_if-\u003evgic_lr[i];\n  }\n```\n\nConsidering that we have many places to plain access shared kvm/kvm_vcpu, I feel we need a separate fix serial to handle that.\n\nWhile for this one, as we cannot use READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE for structure, we can use memcpy() to copy from host to hyp and from hyp to host. The memcpy is implemented by memcpy_64.S which use `rep movsb` based on gemini:\n\n\u003eBypasses Compiler Optimizations: Because rep movsb is a single string-copy hardware instruction, the C compiler (GCC/Clang) has no visibility into how bytes are moved. It cannot optimize the loop away, it cannot perform \"dead store elimination,\" and it cannot merge adjacent writes into a wider register format that over-writes adjacent fields.\n\n\u003ePrevents Host Memory from Being a Scratchpad: Since the compiler is completely blind to the interior mechanics of the assembly block, it is legally blocked from using any destination memory references (%rdi) as a scratchpad or register spill destination.\n\n\u003eHardware-Enforced Byte Bounds: rep movsb copies exactly %rcx number of bytes. It will not write a single byte more or less, protecting adjacent secure fields from structure-bleeding.\n\nSo can be changed to like this:\n```\n+       struct pkvm_memcache tmp_mc \u003d {0};\n+       int ret;\n+\n+       memcpy(\u0026tmp_mc, host_mc, sizeof(struct pkvm_memcache));\n+\n+       ret \u003d topup_pkvm_memcache(mc, min_pages, admit_host_page,\n+                                 pkvm_virt_to_phys, \u0026tmp_mc);\n+\n+       memcpy(host_mc, \u0026tmp_mc, sizeof(struct pkvm_memcache));\n```",
