      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e It seems the type of x should be scalar type but host_mc isn\u0027t, so using READ_ONCE(*host_mc) will trigger a compiling error?\n\nAh, indeed...\n\nSo we could use READ_ONCE for each field of struct pkvm_memcache one by one... But that would be a bit ugly.\n\nSo maybe yeah, it\u0027s better to use memcpy() as you suggested. Although a small \"problem\" I see with it is that memcpy() doesn\u0027t _explicitly_ guarantee this safe behavior, we are just relying on the fact that currently, on x86, it is implemented fully in assembly so the compiler will not mess with it...\n\n\u003e And also asked her about the security risk without using READ_ONCE, and this seems a valid point:\n\u003e [...] If there is a separate code path vulnerability (like a minor buffer overflow or stack-read bug), the Host can use this control to map out or attack the hypervisor stack structure.\n\nThis sounds like not an actual security issue but just making it easier to exploit some other bug?\n\nBut actually, as I understand, the main reason for using READ_ONCE in such cases is to actually fully prevent TOCTOU/race issues like this. Without READ_ONCE (or more precisely, without `volatile`, which READ_ONCE relies on), we have no guarantee that the compiler will generate code that actually reads the value _once_, not multiple times. So, we may be using a temporarily variable to prevent TOCTOU, but the compiler is allowed to stubbornly implement that as loading the data from the host memory every time, instead of remembering it in a temporary storage. (And from what gemini told me, compilers may actually do that in practice, in order to save register space.)\n\n\u003e Considering that we have many places to plain access shared kvm/kvm_vcpu, I feel we need a separate fix serial to handle that.\n\nYeah, agree.\n\n\u003e If so, then seems all the read from shared kvm/kvm_vcpu should use READ_ONCE?\n\nSeems so... Well, _maybe_ in simple cases like reading `vcpu-\u003eguest_debug` in `pkvm_update_exception_bitmap()` (where we just copy the read value to the hypervisor\u0027s data structure and don\u0027t use it after that, we only use it much later) it is not so critical. OTOH, maybe even in such trivial cases it still wouldn\u0027t hurt to use READ_ONCE, for consistency and paranoia?",
